  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Why Leg Acupressure Points Matter</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">The legs carry the body's largest muscles, which makes them one of the most practical regions for learning acupressure. ST36 (Zusanli), the famous "Leg Three Miles," sits just below the knee and is regularly cited in routines for digestion, fatigue, and immune support in the TCM sense. SP6 (Sanyinjiao), where three yin meridians meet above the ankle, is a frequent companion point for menstrual health, sleep, and digestion. Farther down the outer calf, GB34 (Yanglingquan) and GB39 (Xuanzhong) are common in traditional work on muscle, joint, and sciatica-type discomfort, while LV3 (Taichong) on the top of the foot closes the set as a classic for stress and headaches. These points keep reappearing in our digestion, sleep, women's health, and sciatica guides.</p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Meridian lines from the Stomach, Spleen, Liver, Gallbladder, and Bladder all pass through the calf and thigh, which is how classical practice connects leg points to digestion, energy, and sleep. Physically, the points sit in the bellies of large muscles — ST36 in the tibialis anterior, SP6 just behind the shinbone — so slow pressure works on local tightness as well as the deep fascia of the lower limb. That combination makes the legs a favorite zone for both beginners and experienced self-care.</p>
  </section>

