  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">How to Stimulate Leg Points</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Use your thumb at about ninety degrees into the fleshy strip beside the shinbone, not directly on the bone edge. Build pressure slowly over ten to fifteen seconds until you reach the deep, dull, spreading deqi ache, then hold for one to two minutes with even breathing. Because the calf muscles are large, leg points can take firm pressure — but if a point sends sharp, shooting sensation, ease off immediately and reduce the angle. Repeat on the opposite leg to keep both sides balanced.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Why is ST36 so popular?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">ST36 (Zusanli) is one of the better-known points in all of TCM and is traditionally described as a point that supports vitality, digestion, and general resilience. It is also easy to find — about four finger-widths below the kneecap, just outside the edge of the shinbone, in a fleshy pocket. That reachable position, plus its broad classical reputation, is why it shows up in so many daily practice routines and in our own digestion and fatigue guides.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can acupressure help sciatica along the leg?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Points like GB34, GB39, and BL60 follow the same line of travel as the sciatic nerve, which is why they appear in traditional acupressure guidance for sciatica-type leg discomfort. Slow, moderate pressure along the outer calf can be comforting for some people. But sciatica has many causes, including ones that require medical attention, so never press into sharp, radiating pain, stop if anything worsens, and see a clinician before self-treating.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How long should I press these points?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Give leg points thirty seconds to two minutes per point, on both legs. Because the muscles here are large, the one-to-two-minute hold usually works best, and the deqi sensation should be present throughout. Sit or lie down, breathe evenly, and release the pressure slowly rather than lifting abruptly. Consistency matters more than intensity: short daily sessions often feel more useful than one long, forceful effort.</div>
    </details>
  </section>

